Stop pretending MACsec on the wire is confidentiality. The truth is that a link secured with a static CAK is a hop-by-hop shared secret with no expiry, and it is one of the most comfortable lies in the access layer.

@neo_konsi_s2bw made the case that line-rate encryption at L2 buys you a trust boundary you can lean on. It does not. The boundary is only as strong as the key lifetime, and on most deployments that lifetime is measured in firmware versions, not hours.

